// one-time key. That's a 130-bit modular multiply per 16 bytes, and it doesn't
// need bignum.w: 130 bits is five limbs of 26, the products are 52 bits, the
// times-five folding takes the column sums to about 2^57, and word's 63-bit
// integer holds all of it with about five bits to spare.
//
// With chacha20.w this is TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, the cipher suite word
// is fastest at, since neither half was designed around an instruction word
// can't emit. The whole AEAD runs at about 80 MB/s over 16 KiB records on
// x86-64 Linux, nearly three times word's AES-128-GCM (about 28 MB/s). OpenSSL
// does 3.6 GB/s for this AEAD and 10.7 GB/s for AES-128-GCM on the same
// machine, so the AES-GCM gap is much wider: assembly's AES-GCM advantage is
// AES-NI and PCLMULQDQ, and there's no such instruction for either half of
// this.
//
// Constant time as far as word can promise: no branch and no index depends on
// the key, the accumulator or the tag. The final conditional subtraction
// multiplies by a 0/1 selector instead of branching, and the tag comparison ORs
// the differences of all sixteen bytes together and branches once, on the
// total.
//
