// 12.2).

// ---- the bounds a fetch runs under (SPEC 12.2) -----------------------------
//
// A client that dials out to a peer it doesn't control has to be able to give
// up. Without a read deadline, a server that accepts the connection and says
// nothing holds the program as long as it likes, and without a ceiling, a body
// the peer keeps sending is read until memory runs out. A wedged server does
// either by accident, and the caller can't recover, since no failure ever comes
// back.
//
// Four limits here, because they fail differently:
//
//   the idle deadline   SO_RCVTIMEO/SO_SNDTIMEO, so one read or write can't
//                       block for ever. A peer that stalls mid-response trips
//                       this and the read ends.
//   the total deadline  measured across the whole fetch, from the call to the
//                       last byte, because a peer that sends one byte every
//                       twenty seconds never trips the idle deadline and still
//                       never finishes. `tend` carries it into every read and
//                       write the fetch makes (the handshake's as well as the
//                       response's, and every recv inside one TLS record, since
//                       a trickled record is still a trickle), and each of them
//                       is brought in to end at `tend` (dns_await).
//   the body ceiling    the most a response may occupy before the fetch is
//                       abandoned. The arena doesn't shrink, so "read until it
//                       stops" would be a memory bound set by the peer.
//   the flight ceiling  the same bound one layer down: the most the server's
//                       encrypted handshake flight may occupy before the
//                       handshake is abandoned (net_max_flight). A Certificate
//                       may declare 2^24-1 bytes and a server may send record
//                       after record with no Finished.
